ility and this so I would  encourage those of you interested in  questions of provenance and standards to  get involved in groups in RDA or other  groups like that there's a there's  national groups in addition to the  international  before we reinvent the wheel too many  more times because we've done a lot of  reinvention here one final thing in our  last couple of minutes here is maybe the  each of you could comment on the role of  data sharing of provenance and the data  science behind it all in education and  training at our universities and schools  of higher learning and the role that  that plays in training our next  generation of scientists I think it's  kind of an auspicious question because  we are on the eve of some of the marches  for science which are going to be  occurring around the country and I think  that that's probably an important  message to to convey I was curious if  either of you had thoughts on that  subject yes some currently this class  has been teaching for a long time which  is data management and curation and  policies for research data as a PhD  student and hopefully for future faculty  and this this field i really believe  strongly in the role of education and  educating researchers in understanding  and all all the practices and the  policies around data sharing use and how  important it is to make sure that their  science that they're conducting is open  it's not just a matter of believing in  open science but you really have to know  also how to do it you really have to  know how to curate the data what's the  metadata schema what are all the  requirements from the founders and so on  so this is what Chris is teaching in her  classes and I think the students here at  the information studies school they're  very interested in this topic and they  are you know we often talk about how  this should be a mandatory class for all  like undergrad or maybe for graduate  students when they start approaching the  research context yes and we 